<v Chana Joffe-Walt>天啊。

00:46:49.190 --> 00:47:07.970
<v Teresa Hill>Yeah. I was typing away. I would stop. I would go back, type again. And like, unfortunately, I couldn't just go back a little bit. So I literally, when I went to restart, I would have to listen to the message from the very beginning all the way through again. I mean, man, it was brutal.
<v Teresa Hill>是啊。我在努力打字。我会停下来。我会回去，再打一次。不幸的是，我不能只退回一点点。所以我真的，当我重新开始时，我必须从头到尾再听一遍留言。我的意思是，老兄，这太残酷了。

00:47:07.970 --> 00:47:18.560
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But she kept going all the way through the end. She could get this under control. She could create a useful document. And I'm not talking about a simple transcript.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但她一直坚持到底。她能控制这一切。她能创建一个有用的文档。我说的不是简单的转录。

00:47:18.560 --> 00:47:26.600
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Tell me about the format. This is a spreadsheet. Why did you choose to do it this way?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>告诉我格式。这是一个电子表格。你为什么选择这样做？

00:47:26.600 --> 00:47:32.190
<v Teresa Hill>I guess I'm a math teacher and I like spreadsheets more than I like Word. [LAUGHS]
<v Teresa Hill>我想我是个数学老师，我比Word更喜欢电子表格。[笑]

00:47:32.190 --> 00:47:37.640
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It has columns. You say the message number, the time, the date, the phone number of the person.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>它有列。你标出留言号码、时间、日期、那个人的电话号码。

00:47:37.640 --> 00:47:38.540
<v Teresa Hill>Yeah.
<v Teresa Hill>是啊。

00:47:38.540 --> 00:47:49.840
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It feels to me like, oh, when I saw it, I was like, of course she's a principal. She has organized, clear information. Did it feel like you were interacting with it in the way that you might a school budget or--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我感觉就像，哦，当我看到它时，我就想，她当然是个校长。她有组织、清晰的信息。你是否觉得你在以处理学校预算的方式与之互动，或者——

00:47:49.840 --> 00:48:19.210
<v Teresa Hill>Yeah, I mean, I even highlighted so you could see, this is a direct threat. This is an implied threat. I just felt like it was a lot more organized this way. And I think that's part of why I did it. It's something I can control, getting the phone number, writing down what is exactly said. I think that transcribing actually, it helped me, like, detach myself from what was being said and make it more like a job. I don't know if that makes any sense.
<v Teresa Hill>是啊，我的意思是，我甚至做了高亮，这样你能看到，这是一个直接威胁。这是一个暗示的威胁。我只是觉得它用这种方式更有条理。我想这就是我这样做的部分原因。这是我可以控制的事情，获取电话号码，写下确切说了什么。我想转录实际上，它帮助我，就像，让我从所说的事情中抽离出来，让它更像是一份工作。我不知道这有没有意义。

00:48:19.210 --> 00:48:24.020
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>That makes so much sense. That makes so much sense. Yeah.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这太有意义了。这太有意义了。是啊。

00:48:24.020 --> 00:48:56.890
<v Ira Glass>Principal Hill sent her spreadsheet to the superintendent, noting the threats that may still need investigating by police, helpfully color coded by degree of danger, the same way she may have shared, say, a color chart that allows you to assess and visualize student achievement. It's not an obvious choice to respond this way. But everything she did in response to these messages felt so in line with who she is and how she operates.
<v Ira Glass>Hill校长把她的电子表格发给了学监，指出警察可能还需要调查这些威胁，并根据危险程度贴心地进行了颜色编码，就像她可能会分享，比如说，一份允许你评估和可视化学生成绩的颜色图表一样。以这种方式回应并不是一个显而易见的选择。但她回应这些留言所做的一切都与她的个性和运作方式非常一致。

00:48:56.890 --> 00:49:12.760
<v Ira Glass>She managed it. She contained the problem and shielded everyone else from unnecessary distress. She didn't share the messages with anyone else-- her colleagues, her husband. She told her family she'd gotten some threats and to be on alert for strange cars.
<v Ira Glass>她管理了它。她控制了问题，并保护了其他人免受不必要的痛苦。她没有与任何人分享这些留言——她的同事、她的丈夫。她告诉她的家人她收到了一些威胁，要注意奇怪的车。

00:49:12.760 --> 00:49:34.930
<v Ira Glass>And she began parking in the back of the school and entering that way. She told me she imagined if someone was waiting to snipe her out when she arrived for work in the morning, they'd be sitting by the front door. She said this casually, not like a person who was panicked or scared, but a person who was taking the appropriate steps to deal with the problem. It was handled.
<v Ira Glass>她开始把车停在学校后面，并从那里进入。她告诉我她想象如果有人在她早上上班时等着狙击她，他们会坐在前门。她随口说了这些，不是像一个惊慌失措或害怕的人，而是像一个正在采取适当步骤处理问题的人。它被处理了。

00:49:34.930 --> 00:49:48.370
<v Ira Glass>And then we get to the choice that I did not understand. Principal Hill quit in the middle of the school year. Just six months after she was named Principal of the Year, she left her job.
<v Ira Glass>然后我们来到了我不理解的选择。Hill校长在学年中途辞职了。就在她被评为年度校长六个月后，她离开了她的工作。

00:49:48.370 --> 00:49:56.920
<v Ira Glass>She didn't leave immediately after the harassment. She kept going to work for a couple of months. But the voicemails started to get to her.
<v Ira Glass>她没有在骚扰后立即离开。她继续工作了几个月。但语音留言开始影响她了。

00:49:56.920 --> 00:50:14.860
<v Ira Glass>The thing about listening to something over and over is that it stays with you. That was the accidental side effect of transcribing these messages. Principal Hill says the words became ingrained in her head. And she would find herself in a meeting or supervising lunch outside and they'd pop back up.
<v Ira Glass>听东西听多了，它就会留在你心里。那是转录这些留言的意外副作用。Hill校长说这些话在她脑海里根深蒂固了。她会发现自己在开会或者在外面监督午餐时，它们就会冒出来。

00:50:14.860 --> 00:50:27.040
<v Teresa Hill>You know, I'd find myself in the courtyard, like, not talking to anybody, you know? Just like in my own little world sitting there by myself, not interacting with kids and doing the things that I like to do.
<v Teresa Hill>你知道，我会发现自己在庭院里，就像，不跟任何人说话，你知道吗？就像在自己的小世界里独自坐着，不与孩子互动，也不做我喜欢做的事情。

00:50:27.040 --> 00:50:39.000
<v Teresa Hill>I'm a pretty positive person. And I like to keep things light and move forward. And this was like heavy on me.
<v Teresa Hill>我是一个很积极的人。我喜欢让事情轻松并继续前进。但这对我来说感觉很沉重。

00:50:39.000 --> 00:50:40.900
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You couldn't move forward.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你无法继续前进。

00:50:40.900 --> 00:50:58.720
<v Teresa Hill>At the time, I couldn't, no. And I had a hard time. Because you know deep down how you feel. You don't feel good. And, yet, it's not healthy for anyone in your organization to know how you feel. So you slap on a smile--
<v Teresa Hill>当时，我不能，没有。我很难过。因为你知道内心深处你的感觉。你感觉不好。然而，让组织里的任何人知道你的感受对谁都不健康。所以你脸上挂上微笑——

00:50:58.720 --> 00:50:59.520
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Wait, why? Why?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>等等，为什么？为什么？

00:50:59.520 --> 00:51:01.570
<v Teresa Hill>You know, you put on a happy face and--
<v Teresa Hill>你知道，你挂上一张快乐的脸，然后——

00:51:01.570 --> 00:51:06.850
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What is that? That's some principal thing that people can't know how you feel?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>那是什么？那是某种校长的事情，人们不能知道你的感受？

00:51:06.850 --> 00:51:24.870
<v Teresa Hill>Yeah, I guess so. I mean, people want leaders who are strong but who aren't emotional. And, you know, I don't-- I don't ever want to put my own burdens and my own challenges on my people.
<v Teresa Hill>是啊，我想是吧。我的意思是，人们想要强大的领导者，但不是情绪化的。而且，你知道，我不想——我永远不想把我的负担和挑战放在我的人民身上。

00:51:24.870 --> 00:51:47.860
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Dr. Hill is so good at keeping what she feels to herself that even after we talked about this for hours, I still didn't understand why she had to leave, why this was the thing that she couldn't shake off. But then, months after she left her job, Dr. Hill gave testimony in court. And she spoke candidly in public about her experience of all this for the first time.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Hill博士非常善于把自己的感受藏在心里，以至于即使我们讨论了几个小时，我仍然不明白她为什么必须离开，为什么这是她无法摆脱的事情。但在她离开工作岗位几个月后，Hill博士在法庭上作了证。她第一次公开坦率地谈论了她对这一切的经历。

00:51:47.860 --> 00:51:53.620
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The parents who wouldn't leave her school were charged with trespassing. There was a whole trial. They were found guilty.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>那些不愿离开她学校的家长被指控非法入侵。进行了整个审判。他们被判有罪。

00:51:53.620 --> 00:52:08.790
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And then the judge asked Dr. Hill if she wanted to say anything. Did she want to share the effect this had on her? And she did. She talked about her fear. And this time, she wasn't all casual in describing avoiding the front entrance of the school.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>然后法官问Hill博士她想说什么吗。她想分享这给她带来的影响吗？她说了。她谈到了她的恐惧。这一次，在描述避开学校正门时，她一点也不随意。

00:52:08.790 --> 00:52:31.930
<v Teresa Hill>I couldn't go through the front of the school in this day and age with shootings and attacks on public officials. If you listen to the messages and you heard their anger and their blood pressure raised, I feared for my safety and I feared for the safety of my family. This has negatively impacted me physically.
<v Teresa Hill>在当今枪击和攻击公职人员的时代，我无法穿过学校的前门。如果你听了那些留言，听到了他们的愤怒和他们血压的升高，我为我的安全感到恐惧，我为我的家人的安全感到恐惧。这在身体上对我产生了负面影响。

00:52:31.930 --> 00:52:51.790
<v Teresa Hill>I've had health problems because of this. It's impacted me mentally. And, unfortunately, the biggest impact is it affected me professionally. Never in my 29 years as an educator would I think of leaving my school halfway through the year.
<v Teresa Hill>因为这个，我的健康出现了问题。它在精神上影响了我。而且，不幸的是，最大的影响是它在专业上影响了我。在我29年的教学生涯中，我从来没想过会在一年中途离开学校。

00:52:51.790 --> 00:52:56.410
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>She said to the judge, you have to understand what I had to listen to.
